You can use the `focus()` method on the element found by `getElementsByTagName`. Here's an example:
```
const elements = document.getElementsByTagName("input");
const element = elements[0]; // or elements[1], etc.

element.focus();
```
This will focus on the first `input` element found on the page.

If you want to focus on a specific element, you can loop through the elements array and check if the element's name, id, or class matches the one you're looking for:
```
const elements = document.getElementsByTagName("input");

for (let i = 0; i < elements.length; i++) {
  const element = elements[i];
  if (element.name === "myInputName") {
    element.focus();
    break;
  }
}
```
Note that `getElementsByTagName` returns a NodeList, which is a live collection of elements. This means that if you create a reference to an element in the NodeList, it will still be valid even if the element is removed or added to the DOM later.<|start_header_id|>assistant<|end_header_id|>

